f which are\n   sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n   least one of them.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), these are\nthe only possible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Reduction to a triangular set)\n\nLet \\(v,h\\) be non\u2011negative integers with \\(v+h=n-k\\).  \nConsider the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v\n\\]\n\nand the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is not on any of them iff  \n\n\\[\na>v\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b>h .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-v,\\;b'=b-h\\); then \\(a',b'>0\\) and\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\iff a'+b'\\le (v+h)+k+1\\iff a'+b'\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the set of points not covered by the \\(v+h=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k:=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe right\u2011isosceles lattice triangle of size \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny covering of a triangle)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose\nunion contains all points of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base cases.*  \n\n* \\(m=0\\): \\(\\mathcal T_0=\\varnothing\\); the statement is vacuous.  \n* \\(m=1\\): \\(\\mathcal T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny\nand passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim holds for all integers \\(<m\\) with \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\mid a\\le m-1\\},\\\\\nB &=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\mid b\\le m-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are copies of \\(\\mathcal T_{m-1}\\); they intersect in the\nsingle point \\((m-1,1)=(1,m-1)\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exist \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_A\\) covering \\(A\\) and \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_B\\) covering \\(B\\).  All lines in \\(\\mathcal L_A\\cup\\mathcal L_B\\)\nare distinct except possibly one common line: the line through the two\nintersection points \\((m-1,1)\\) and \\((1,m-1)\\), namely  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:\\; x+y=m,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  Consequently\n\\(\\ell_{-1}\\) cannot belong to either \\(\\mathcal L_A\\) or \\(\\mathcal L_B\\); the\ntwo families are actually disjoint, giving \\(2(m-1)\\) sunny lines.\n\nNow replace the two lines that pass through the outer\u2011diagonal points\n\\((m,1)\\) and \\((1,m)\\) (these two points are not yet covered, because the\nonly line that could contain both has slope \\(-1\\)) by the single sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{m+2}{2}.\n\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_m\\) contains both \\((m,1)\\) and \\((1,m)\\); its slope\n\\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  All other\npoints of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) remain covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_A\\cup\n\\mathcal L_B\\).  After this replacement we have exactly\n\n\\[\n(m-1)+(m-1)-1+1 = m\n\\]\n\ndistinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_m\\).  Thus the statement holds for\n\\(m\\).\n\nBy induction the lemma is true for every \\(m\\ge 0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nChoose any pair \\((v,h)\\) of non\u2011negative integers with \\(v+h=n-k\\) (for\ninstance \\(v=0,\\;h=n-k\\)).  Draw the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\)\nand the \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\); all are non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on these \\(v+h=n-k\\) lines\nform exactly the triangle \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  Lemma\u202f2 provides a family of\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\nHence we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least\none of the lines.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), these are\nprecisely the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
