n\n   Write \\(s_i\\) for the slope of \\(L_i\\).  \n   Because the two points \\((v+1,i)\\) and \\((v+2,i+s_i)\\) are lattice points with horizontal distance \\(1\\), every \\(s_i\\) is an **integer**.\n\n4. **What the next columns must contain.**  \n   In column \\(x=v+t\\;(t\\ge 2)\\) the required points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(v+t,y)\\mid 1\\le y\\le m+1-t\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For the line \\(L_i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap\\{x=v+t\\}=(v+t,\\;i+(t-1)s_i). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence for each \\(t\\ge 2\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,i+(t-1)s_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\supseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,m+1-t\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **At most one sunny line for \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   *Definition.*  A line is **non‑sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n   Assume that at least two of the slopes \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_m\\) are not in \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{i\\mid s_i\\ge 1\\},\\qquad N=\\{i\\mid s_i\\le -2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slopes are integers, \\(P\\cup N\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(|P|+|N|\\ge 2\\).\n\n   Consider the column \\(x=v+2\\) (\\(t=2\\)).  \n   By (4) we need the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).  \n   For \\(i\\in P\\) we have \\(i+s_i\\ge i+1\\); for \\(i\\in N\\) we have \\(i+s_i\\le i-2\\); for a non‑sunny line we have \\(i+s_i\\in\\{i,i-1\\}\\).\n\n   Hence the values contributed by the sunny lines lie **strictly** outside the interval \\([i,i]\\) for their own index \\(i\\).  \n   Consequently each sunny line *removes* at least one integer from the interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) that would otherwise be supplied by a non‑sunny line.\n\n   Because we have exactly \\(m\\) lines but only \\(m-1\\) integers to realise in this column, **exactly one** integer may be missing.  \n   Therefore **at most one** line can have a slope outside \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n\n   The same reasoning applied to the next column \\(x=v+3\\) (where we need the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m-2\\}\\)) shows that the conclusion does not depend on the particular column: the requirement that each column loses precisely one point forces the number of sunny lines to be \\(\\le 1\\).\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realising the bounds for \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   All three directions are non‑sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Keep the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and replace the diagonal line by any sunny line through the point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\((n,1)\\)).  \n   Exactly one line is sunny and the covering property is unchanged.\n\n   Thus for \\(n\\ge 4\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n7. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   Here \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=4\\) are all non‑sunny and cover the six points.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Replace the diagonal line by the sunny line \\(y=x\\); together with the two vertical lines we obtain a covering with exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y-1 = 1\\,(x-1)      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y-3 = -2\\,(x-1)    &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Suppose three lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \n   Because there are six points and three lines, each line must contain exactly two of the points.  \n   The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) lie in the same column; by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of them belong to the