\\{(v+2,y)\\mid 1\\le y\\le m-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy (2) the intersection of \\(L_i\\) with this column is \\((v+2,i+s_i)\\);\nconsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\{i+s_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\supseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf for some index \\(i\\) the number \\(i+s_i\\) belongs to the interval\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\), then \\(s_i=(i+s_i)-i\\) is an integer.  Thus all\nslopes are integers except possibly for a single index \\(i_0\\) for which\n\\(i_0+s_{i_0}\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).\n\nAssume such an index \\(i_0\\) exists.  For the column \\(x=v+3\\) (\\(t=3\\))\nthe required points have \\(y\\)-coordinates \\(1,\\dots ,m-2\\).  From (2) we\nobtain the intersection  \n\n\\[\nL_{i_0}\\cap\\{x=v+3\\}=(v+3,i_0+2s_{i_0}).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s_{i_0}\\) is not an integer, the number \\(2s_{i_0}\\) cannot be an\ninteger; otherwise \\(s_{i_0}\\) would be a half‑integer and then\n\\(i_0+s_{i_0}\\) would be a half‑integer, contradicting its non‑integrality.\nHence \\(i_0+2s_{i_0}\\) is not an integer, so \\(L_{i_0}\\) does not meet the\ncolumn \\(x=v+3\\) at any required point.  The same argument applied to the\ncolumn \\(x=v+t\\) for any \\(t\\ge2\\) shows that \\(L_{i_0}\\) never contributes\nto any column of \\(C\\) beyond \\(x=v+1\\).\n\nBut the column \\(x=v+m\\) contains the single point \\((v+m,1)\\); some\nnon‑vertical line must pass through it, which is impossible for \\(L_{i_0}\\).\nThus the exceptional index cannot exist, and all slopes \\(s_i\\) are\nintegers. ∎\n\nFrom now on we assume \\(s_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\) for all \\(i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Points in later columns\n\nSubstituting \\(x=v+t\\) into (2) gives, for any integer \\(t\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap\\{x=v+t\\}=\\bigl(v+t,\\;i+(t-1)s_i\\bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence for each \\(t\\ge2\\) the covering condition for the column\n\\(x=v+t\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i+(t-1)s_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\supseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,m+1-t