-------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Realising the bounds for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n  The diagonal has slope \\(-1\\); all lines are non‑sunny and they cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Keep the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and replace the diagonal line by any sunny line through the point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\((n,1)\\)).  \n  Exactly one line is sunny and the covering property is unchanged.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=4\\) cover all six points.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Replace the diagonal line by the sunny line \\(y=x\\); together with the two vertical lines we obtain a covering with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y-1 = 1\\,(x-1)      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y-3 = -2\\,(x-1)    &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n  Suppose that exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  Then there is exactly one non‑sunny line.  \n  A non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) lie in the same column.  A non‑vertical line meets this column in at most one point, so to cover all three points the unique non‑sunny line must be **vertical**, namely the line \\(x