 and together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Suppose three lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \n   Because there are six points and three lines, each line must contain exactly two of the points.  \n   The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) lie in the same column; by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of them belong to the same line, which must therefore be vertical and hence non‑sunny.  \n   The remaining vertical line can contain at most one of the points of the first row \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); any line joining the leftover point of the first column to a point of the first row is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently at least two of the three lines are non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that exactly two are sunny.  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\nCombining the cases \\(n\\ge 4\\) and \\(n=3\\) yields the final answer stated in the theorem.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Justification Gaps (need more detail):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"Step 3 – “every \\(s_i\\) is an integer.”\"\n   **Issue**: Justification Gap – the integrality of the slopes is asserted without proof; it follows only after invoking the covering condition (4), which is not referenced here.\n\n2. **Location**: \"Step 5 – “At most one sunny line for \\(n\\ge4\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Justification Gap – the counting argument (“each sunny line removes at least one integer… exactly one integer may be missing”) is informal and does not rigorously show that two or more sunny lines cannot satisfy condition (4). The deduction that \\(|P|+|N|\\ge2\\) forces a contradiction is not demonstrated with a complete combinatorial argument.\n\n3. **Location**: \"Step 7 – “Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: Justification Gap – the proof assumes that every line must contain exactly two points and that there are exactly two vertical lines, which is not justified. The pigeon‑hole reasoning is incomplete, and the conclusion that at least two lines must be non‑sunny is not rigorously established.\n\n\n### Your Task ###\n\n**DO NOT re-solve the problem from scratch.** Your answer is already correct.\n\n**DO:** Make TARGETED FIXES to address each issue above:\n\n1. **For Critical Errors (notation/logic):**\n   - Fix the exact statement that has the error\n   - Use correct mathematical notation\n   - Example: If \"PA·PQ\" should be \"PA·PE\", change it precisely\n\n2. **For Justification Gaps:**\n   - ADD intermediate steps to show WHY the claim is true\n   - Provide algebraic verification (e.g., substitute and simplify)\n   - Example: If claiming \"distance = radius\", show the calculation\n\n3. **Preserve everything else:**\n   - Keep the same proof structure and approach\n   - Don't change your answer (LOCKED)\n   - Only fix the flagged issues\n\n### Output Format ###\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Format your response with the exact structure below:\n\n```\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n[Your complete refined proof here, with:]\n- All issues from verification feedback addressed\n- Intermediate steps filled in for gaps\n- Correct notation for errors\n- Same answer in \\boxed{} at the end\n```\n\n**Critical**: Start your response with EXACTLY \"### Detailed Solution ###\" (this marker is required for the verification system).\n\n**Remember**: Your answer is CORRECT. This is proof refinement, not problem solving.\n\n**Note**: This is refinement round 4. Previous rounds found similar issues. Focus on COMPLETE fixes this time.\n"
