                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\nWe determine all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a convenient configuration  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines among the \\(n\\) lines,  \n* \\(m=n-v\\) = number of non‑vertical lines.\n\nIn column \\(x=a\\) there are exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nA non‑vertical line can meet a given column in at most one point, therefore any column that contains more than \\(m\\) points must be covered by a vertical line.  \nConsequently the first \\(v\\) columns, i.e. the columns  \n\n\\[\nx=1,2,\\dots ,v,\n\\]\n\nare vertical, and the remaining columns are  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{v+1,v+2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |C|=m .\n\\]\n\nIn column \\(x=v+1\\) the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(v+1,i)\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m).\n\\]\n\nSince there are exactly \\(m\\) non‑vertical lines, each of them contains exactly one of these points.  \nAfter a suitable renumbering we may assume  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\text{ contains }(v+1,i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(s_i\\) for the slope of \\(L_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Integrality of the slopes  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  For every \\(i\\) we have \\(s_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L_i\\) is not vertical, it meets the hypotenuse line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the boundary of the triangular set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)) at a unique point.  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on that hypotenuse are precisely  \n\n\\[\n(v+1+t,\\;m-t)\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,m-1),\n\\]\n\nall of which have integer coordinates.  \nLet \\(t_i\\) be the (non‑negative) integer such that \\(L_i\\) meets the hypotenuse at \\((v+1+t_i,\\;m-t_i)\\).  \nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m-t_i-i}{t_i}=s_i .\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are integers, so \\(s_i\\) is rational.  \nMoreover, using the equation of the line we have  \n\n\\[\ni+(t_i-1)s_i=m-t_i . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the right–hand side of (2) is an integer and \\(t_i-1\\) is an integer, \\((t_i-1)s_i\\) is an integer; hence \\(s_i\\) itself must be an integer. ∎\n\nThus every non‑vertical line has an **integer** slope.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of the non‑vertical lines  \n\nFrom (1) and the definition of slope we obtain for any integer \\(t\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap\\{x=v+t\\}=\\bigl(v+t,\\;i+(t-1)s_i\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(t\\ge2\\) the required points in column \\(x=v+t\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(v+t,y)\\mid 1\\le y\\le m+1-t\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence for each \\(t\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i+(t-1)s_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\supseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,m+1-t\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  At most one sunny line when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nRecall that a line is **non‑sunny** precisely when its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\); all other slopes (including the vertical ones already counted in \\(v\\)) are sunny.\n\nConsider the column \\(x=v+2\\) (i.e. \\(t=2\\)).  \nBy (5) we need the set  \n\n\\[\nY_2:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\na_i:=i+s_i .\n\\]\n\nThe covering condition for this column reads  \n\n\\[\nY_2\\subseteq\\{a_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|Y_2|=m-1\\) and we have exactly \\(m\\) numbers \\(a_i\\), **at most one** of the \\(a_i\\) can lie outside \\(Y_2\\).\n\n*If \\(L_i\\) is non‑sunny* then \\(s_i\\in\\{0,-1\\}\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\na_i\\in\\{i,i-1\\}\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L_i\\) is sunny* then \\(s_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); hence \\(a_i\\neq i\\) and \\(a_i\\neq i-1\\).  \nThus a sunny line contributes a value that is **not** of the form (7).  \nThere are two possibilities:\n\n1. \\(a_i\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}\\). Then \\(a_i\\) is automatically outside \\(Y_2\\).\n\n2. \\(a_i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) but \\(a_i\\neq i,i-1\\). In this case \\(a_i\\) must coincide with some other \\(a_j\\); otherwise we would obtain a new integer in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,m\\}\\) that is not needed, forcing **two** integers to be missing from \\(Y_2\\), contradicting the fact that only one integer may be missing.\n\nTherefore a sunny line can be the **unique** line whose contribution lies outside \\(Y_2\\), and a second sunny line would force at least two values to be missing from \\(Y_2\\), which is impossible. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge4. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Realising the bounds for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n  The diagonal line has slope \\(-1\\); hence all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny and they cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Keep the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and replace the diagonal line by any sunny line through the point \\((n,1)\\); for instance the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Exactly one line is sunny and the covering property is unchanged because \\((n,1)\\) is the only point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying in the last column.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Realising \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x+y=4\n\\]\n\ncover all six points.\n\n*Realising \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the diagonal line \\(x+y=4\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\); together with the two vertical lines we obtain a covering with exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Realising \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1 = 1\\,(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-3 = -2\\,(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume that three lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nLet the non‑sunny line be \\(L\\).  Since a non‑sunny line must be vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\), we examine the three possibilities.\n\n1. **\\(L\\) vertical.**  \n   *If \\(L:x=1\\)*, it contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n   The remaining points \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \n   Any line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) is vertical (non‑sunny);  \n   any line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) is horizontal (non‑sunny);  \n   the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   Hence no sunny line can contain any of the remaining points – a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(L:x=2\\)*, it contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n   The three points of the first column \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) cannot be covered by a single sunny line (a sunny line cannot be vertical), so at least two lines are needed for that column, leaving no line for the point \\((3,1)\\).  Contradiction.\n\n2. **\\(L\\) horizontal.**  \n   Then \\(L\\) must be \\(y=1\\), covering \\((2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n   The four points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) remain.  \n   As before, the three points of the first column need at least two distinct lines, and the point \\((2,2)\\) cannot be placed on either of those lines without producing a non‑sunny slope (the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) has slope \\(1\\) – sunny, but then we would need a third line for \\((1,2)\\) or \\((1,3)\\)).  Hence two sunny lines are insufficient.\n\n3. **\\(L\\) of slope \\(-1\\).**  \n   Then \\(L\\) is the line \\(x+y=4\\), which contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   The two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) cannot lie on a sunny line together (the line through them is vertical).  \n   Thus they must belong to distinct lines, and the third point \\((2,1)\\) cannot be placed on either of those two lines without giving a non‑sunny slope (the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is horizontal, the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently a third sunny line would be required, contradicting the assumption that only two lines are sunny.\n\nIn every case a configuration with exactly two sunny lines leads to a contradiction; therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the analysis for \\(n\\ge4\\) with the special case \\(n=3\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines. ∎"
